hted  some of the real problems how can an  officer with that many citizen  complaints  uh be out teaching other officers we  know  that our shared values accountability  and transparency  and consequence when you break laws have  to be the core pillars of this  of this law or it's not worth us doing  something  so i i feel that there's a moment in  history right now the white house  has been extraordinarily helpful the  department of justice police leadership  uh and especially civil rights groups  so again um i know that this is a moment  we haven't had meaningful police reform  uh in this country for decades and there  clearly as we see this drumbeat  of god-awful deaths and violence against  unarmed  african-americans that we all have to  share a collective urgency in this  country  to change laws to expand accountability  and to work  to end a crisis that right here in  washington martin luther king  in his i have a dream speech was  speaking about uh police brutality  the kerner commission and other  commissions decades ago we're pointing  out the very many of the very same  problems we're dealing with now we have  to  do something about this i want to pick  up senator booker what you just said  about you and tim scott  both men of color who have had lived  experiences who have talked about your  encounters with police  how much pressure do you feel either  self-imposed or pressure from the  outside  to get this done this time you know when  george floyd was first murdered  there were calls on both sides everybody  thought this was a moment  finally something will happen and a year  later we are still talking  do you feel a lot of pressure yourself  um i feel the weight of obligation um  i'm going to meet with the floyd family  today he was a son  he was a father he was a brother and  we failed him uh not just derek chauvin  he was to blame but we all are have been  responsible  for having a system in america that has  allowed things like this to happen with  